{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787080180,"nonce":"v1K+8u8ZzbHEqaSL02yVHsU71pM=","title":"`?suppressed_reason=snoozed` is a 422 on the audit filter, so the one suppression a human asked for is the one nobody can query","message":"**What is wrong.** `SuppressedReason` is a closed set of six (`internal/notification/domain/suppression.go:19-35`): `channel_disabled`, `no_policy`, `snoozed`, `throttled`, `verbosity`, `duplicate_render`. The audit filter admits five. `suppressedReasons` (`internal/notification/api/audit.go:42-43`) is `{\"no_policy\", \"throttled\", \"verbosity\", \"channel_disabled\", \"duplicate_render\"}` — `snoozed` is absent, and it is the only member of the domain set the filter cannot name.\n\n**The demonstration.** The gap is not a silent empty page, which would be bad enough; it is a refusal. `audit.go:94` passes the list to `p.EnumCSV(\"suppressed_reason\", suppressedReasons...)`, and `EnumCSV` (`internal/platform/httpx/bind.go:219-235`) records a violation and returns nil the moment a value is not in `allowed`. So `GET /api/v1/notifications?suppressed_reason=snoozed` comes back a validation error reading *\"suppressed_reason must be one of: no_policy, throttled, verbosity, channel_disabled, duplicate_render\"* — a closed-vocabulary message that tells the operator, in oto's own voice, that `snoozed` is not a suppression reason. It is. `notifications_suppmap_ck` has admitted it since migration 00018, `suppressorOrder` (`suppression.go:73-80`) ranks it third of six, and oto writes it.\n\nThe absence is pre-existing and not the storm/flap removal. `audit.go:38-41` says so in the file: *\"⚠️ `snoozed` IS STILL ABSENT AND THAT IS A PRE-EXISTING GAP, NOT THIS CHANGE. Migration 00018 added it to `notifications_suppmap_ck`, oto writes it, and this filter has never been able to select it.\"* The neighbouring note at `:33-37` explains why `storm` and `flapping` are correctly absent — the column can no longer hold them, so a filter value would always return nothing — which is the opposite case and makes the omission of `snoozed` an oversight rather than a policy.\n\n**Why it matters.** `listNotifications`'s own header (`:51-54`) states the promise this breaks: *\"⛔ SUPPRESSED INTENTS ARE IN THIS LIST. Every intent oto formed is here, including the ones it deliberately said nothing about, and why. Suppression is always recorded: damping that cannot be inspected is indistinguishable from a bug.\"* Of the six things oto records about its own quiet, `snoozed` is the one the precedence chain singles out as *\"a DELIBERATE HUMAN ACT, and therefore the most actionable explanation a reader can be given\"* (`suppression.go:45-47`). \"Show me everything oto withheld because somebody snoozed it\" is the single most likely question anyone asks this endpoint, and it is the one question it answers with a 422.\n\n**Where.** `internal/notification/api/audit.go:42-43` (`suppressedReasons`), `:38-41` (the note recording the gap), `:94` (the `EnumCSV` call) · `internal/platform/httpx/bind.go:219-235` (`EnumCSV`, why it is a refusal not an empty page) · `internal/notification/domain/suppression.go:19-35` and `:73-80` (the domain set and its order) · `db/migrations/00018` (`notifications_suppmap_ck`, where the value has been legal all along).\n\n**Scope.** small — one string added to one slice — but it is a widening of a published contract enum, so the OpenAPI spec and whatever `dto-check` compares must move with it.\n\n**Done when.** `suppressedReasons` names all six members of the domain set, and a test derives it from the domain rather than restating it, so a seventh value cannot be added to `SuppressedReason` without the filter learning it. `GET /api/v1/notifications?suppressed_reason=snoozed` returns the snoozed suppressions. The OpenAPI enum for the parameter lists the same six. The warning comment at `audit.go:38-41` is deleted, because the gap it records is closed.","files":null}]}